{"type": "spr", "stem": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\">A right circular cylinder has a height of <math alttext=\"16\"><mn>16</mn>\n</math> inches and a volume of <math alttext=\"3,136 pi\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>3,136</mn>\n\t<mi>&pi;</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> cubic inches. What is the radius, in inches, of this cylinder?</p>", "keys": ["14"], "rationale": "<p>The correct answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"14\"><mn>14</mn></math>. The volume, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper V\"><mi>V</mi></math>, of a right circular cylinder is given by the formula <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper V equals pi r squared h\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>h</mi></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi></math> is the radius of the base of the cylinder and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"h\"><mi>h</mi></math> is the height of the cylinder. It&rsquo;s given that the right circular cylinder has a height of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"16\"><mn>16</mn></math> inches and a volume of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"3,136 pi\"><mn>3,136</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></math> cubic inches. Substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"16\"><mn>16</mn></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"h\"><mi>h</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"3,136 pi\"><mn>3,136</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper V\"><mi>V</mi></math> in the formula <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper V equals pi r squared h\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>h</mi></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"3,136 pi equals pi r squared left parenthesis 16 right parenthesis\"><mn>3,136</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>(</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>)</mo></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"3,136 pi equals 16 pi r squared\"><mn>3,136</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"16 pi\"><mn>16</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"196 equals r squared\"><mn>196</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi></math> represents the radius of the cylinder, the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi></math> is positive. Taking the positive square root of both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r equals 14\"><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn></math>. Therefore, the radius, in inches, of the cylinder is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"14\"><mn>14</mn></math>.</p>", "externalid": "df0a6944-eb80-45dd-890a-e82089329b8e", "correct_answer": ["14"]}